two very different men but united in  their compassion to break the stigma  around living with HIV Gareth Thomas a  former Welsh and British Lions rugby  captain is a legend of his sport two  months ago he made this incredibly  powerful announcement I'm living with  HIV now you have that information that  makes me extremely vulnerable but it  does not make me weak Prince Harry was  among the first to lend support to  Gareth HIV awareness a cause close to  his heart here seen getting publicly  tested for the virus the two men have  since continued to stand together in the  fight to educate people about HIV I did  something I'm really proud of but  there's more I want to do you know I  want to continue that message even  though that message was a lot bigger  than I ever thought it was gonna be here  we still need to get it out to more  people and we still need to get you know  more people I have to warn you might  come out because it's so cold out here  Gareth Thomas mitts he felt trapped and  ashamed after his diagnosis he says  Prince Harry a vocal campaigner about  mental health has been a huge help you  know he's been unbelievably unbelievably  supportive from day one yeah and it kind  of it kind of felt surreal but we had  something in common  because even though you know I'm from a  council estate in Bridgend and and he's  a royalty sometimes then barriers get  broken down when people are both  passionate about something like he  didn't have to phone me he didn't have  to text me to check him okay that's not  a royal duty he did it because he's a  human being who actually cares  they may seem worlds apart but Alfie and  Harry as they call each other are now  close friends sharing a love for rugby  and a strong desire to help others  Charlotte Lomas Sky News
 

